course: religion-ethics
lesson: 23
title: Sunday service, hymns and the Lord's Prayer
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most Christians meet on Sunday, the day of the week when Jesus rose from the dead, to worship God together, because faith is shared and not only private.
explain: A service usually moves through songs, prayers, Bible readings, a sermon that explains a passage and how to live it, often communion, and a blessing that sends everyone out into the week.
explain: Some churches sing old hymns with an organ and a choir, others modern worship songs with a band; «Amazing Grace» was written by John Newton, a former slave-ship captain whose life changed completely.
explain: At the heart of most services is the Lord's Prayer, the prayer Jesus taught: it praises God, asks for daily bread, asks forgiveness «as we forgive», asks for protection, and ends with «Amen», meaning «so be it».
explain: The building helps too: the font for baptism, the pulpit for preaching, the lectern for reading, the altar or communion table, the pews, and often stained glass that tells Bible stories in pictures.
explain: For a child today, a service is a weekly practice of thanking, listening, saying sorry, giving and going back out to live it.
fact: Most Christians worship on Sunday because Jesus rose from the dead on a Sunday.
fact: A service usually has songs, prayers, Bible readings, a sermon, often communion, and a blessing at the end.
fact: The sermon is a talk by the minister explaining a Bible passage and how to live it.
fact: Hymns are songs of worship sung together, such as «Amazing Grace» and «All Things Bright and Beautiful».
fact: «Amazing Grace» was written by John Newton, a former slave-ship captain who changed his life.
fact: Many churches sing modern songs with a band; others have choirs and organs.
fact: The Lord's Prayer is the prayer Jesus taught, beginning «Our Father in heaven, hallowed be your name»; «hallowed» means made holy, treated as holy.
fact: The Lord's Prayer includes praise, asking for daily needs, forgiveness and protection.
fact: The line «forgive us as we forgive» links being forgiven by God with forgiving others.
fact: «Amen» is a Hebrew word said at the end of prayers, meaning «so be it» or «truly».
fact: The collection is an offering of money for the church's work and for people in need.
fact: The font, a big stone bowl for water often near the door, is used for baptism, the pulpit for preaching and the lectern for Bible readings.
fact: The altar or communion table is where the bread and wine are prepared for communion.
fact: Seventh-day Adventists and some other Christians worship on Saturday instead.
exercise: Why do most Christians worship on Sunday? | Jesus rose from the dead on a Sunday | Sunday was the day God made the world | The Romans ordered churches to open then | Sunday is the only day without school
exercise: What usually comes at the very end of a service? | A blessing that sends people out | The first hymn of the morning | The reading of the church notices only | The baptism of every child present
exercise: What is a sermon? | A talk explaining a Bible passage and how to live it | A long song sung by the whole choir at the end of the service | A list of names of people who are ill | A prayer said quietly by each person
exercise: Which of these is a well-known hymn? | Amazing Grace | Happy Birthday | Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star | Jingle Bells
exercise: Who wrote «Amazing Grace»? | John Newton, a former slave-ship captain | King David, when he was a young shepherd boy | Saint Paul, while he was chained up in prison | A choir of monks in a monastery in old Ireland
exercise: Why is it powerful that a slave-ship captain wrote «Amazing Grace»? | It shows a person can be forgiven and change | It shows sailors were the best singers | It shows slavery was not really wrong in those days | It shows hymns must be about the sea
exercise: Why do churches worship in such different styles? | Different styles can all honour God | Only the newest style is allowed now | Organs are banned in modern churches | Bands are only for churches abroad
exercise: How does the Lord's Prayer begin? | Our Father in heaven, hallowed be your name | The Lord is my shepherd, I shall not want | In the beginning God created the heavens | Glory to God in the highest heaven
exercise: Who taught the Lord's Prayer? | Jesus | Moses | Saint Peter | The first Pope
exercise: What do Christians ask for with «give us today our daily bread»? | The everyday things they need | A big feast on Sundays only | Bread for the communion service only | Enough money to be rich
exercise: Why might «forgive us as we forgive» be hard to pray honestly? | You ask God to forgive you the way you forgive others | It is in an old language nobody understands | It is only for priests to say aloud | You have to say it twice in one breath
exercise: What does «Amen» mean? | So be it, or truly | Goodbye, see you next week | The end, sit down now | Hello God, are you there
exercise: Why do people say «Amen» together? | To show they agree with the prayer | To tell the vicar to stop talking | To wake up anyone who fell asleep | Because it is the name of an angel
exercise: What is the collection for? | The church's work and people in need | Paying the vicar for every single sermon | Buying sweets for the children at Sunday school | A fine for anyone who comes to church late
exercise: What is the font used for? | Baptism | Preaching | Reading the Bible | Communion
exercise: What is the pulpit for? | Preaching the sermon | Baptising babies | Keeping the hymn books | Collecting the money
exercise: What do stained glass windows often show? | Bible stories in pictures | The names of the church's donors | Maps of the local streets | The times of the services
test: «church is on saturday duh, it's the sabbath» says your cousin. What is true for most Christians? | Most worship on Sunday, the day Jesus rose | All Christians must worship on Saturday | Christians worship only on Christmas Day | Most worship on Friday, the day Jesus died
test: Priya, a Hindu friend visiting church, whispers: «Why's everyone saying amen?» Best answer? | It means so be it; they agree with the prayer | It means the service is finished now and people can go home | It is the name of the church's saint | It means hello in the Bible's language
test: «Ugh, the sermon's just the vicar going on and on,» moans your brother. What is it actually for? | Explaining the Bible and how to live it | Filling up the time before the collection is taken | Showing off how much the vicar knows | Telling people off for missing church
test: Your mate says the band at his church is «proper worship» and your organ is «old rubbish». Best reply? | Different styles can all honour God | He's right, organs do not count | No, only organs are proper worship | Bands are against the Bible
test: «Our father who art in heaven, harold be thy name» sings a little kid. What is the right word? | Hallowed, meaning made holy | Harold, the name of God | Holloway, a place in heaven | Hollow, meaning empty
test: A classmate who doesn't go to church asks: «Do you have to pay to get in?» What's true? | No; the collection is a free gift for the church and the needy | Yes, each person pays a ticket at the door | Yes, children pay half price | No, but you must pay to pray
test: Your friend is still furious at a girl who lied about her, and asks if she can skip the forgive line in the Lord's Prayer. What would a wise Sunday-school teacher say? | Pray it and ask God for help to forgive | Yes, skip any line that feels hard | Say it out loud but keep your fingers crossed behind your back | Only pray it when you feel happy
test: «what's that big stone bowl by the door for, washing ur hands?» Put your friend right. | It is the font, used for baptism | It is a bird bath for the church garden | It is for holy coins from the collection | It is where the choir rinses their cups
test: An olympiad judge asks: «Why might a hymn written by a former slave-trader move people today?» Best answer? | It shows grace can change even someone who did great wrong | It proves slave-trading was acceptable then | It is popular only because the tune is easy | It shows that only sailors understand God
test: «The Lord's Prayer is just asking for stuff,» says an older boy. What is a fuller answer? | It also praises God, asks forgiveness and protection | Yes, it only asks God for things like food, money and presents | No, it only praises God and asks nothing | It is only about forgiving other people
test: A judge follows up: «Why do Christians say faith is shared, not just private?» | They worship and pray together as one family | Praying alone is forbidden in every church | Only groups are allowed to talk to God, never one person alone | Private faith does not count at all
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the majority of Christians assemble for worship upon the first day of the week. | That it is the day upon which Jesus is held to have risen from the dead | That it is the day upon which the world was completed | That it is the day upon which Moses received the Law upon the mountain | That it is the sole day exempt from compulsory education
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a sermon? | An exposition of a scriptural passage and its application to conduct | A choral composition performed by the assembled congregation | An enumeration of parishioners suffering from illness | A silent supplication offered individually by each worshipper
exam: Ascertain the signification of the Hebrew word «Amen» as pronounced at the conclusion of prayer. | So be it, or truly | The service is hereby concluded | Peace be upon you | Glory to God
exam: Select the response consistent with the established purposes of the collection taken during divine service. | The furtherance of the church's work and the relief of persons in need | The remuneration of the minister for each and every sermon he delivers | The imposition of a penalty for late attendance | The procurement of confectionery for the Sunday school
